@* Table's Filtering page, moved from its Overview: the column filters, lists of values, the URL,
   operators, and filters in code. *@

<Heading Level="2">Column filters</Heading>
<TextBlock>A column filter narrows the rows by the values in one column. Set <Code>Filterable</Code> on a column to add a filter icon to its header; clicking the icon opens the filter. How it filters depends on the column's type:</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Column type</th><th>Filter</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Text</td><td>Contains the text, anywhere in the value</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Number</td><td>A range: at least, at most, or between</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Date</td><td>On that day</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Enum or bool</td><td>A list of checkboxes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filters" />
<TextBlock>The icon is marked while its column is filtered, and Clear in the filter removes it.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>To list every active filter in a row below the table, set <Code>FilterPanel</Code>. The user can remove each filter there, or all of them with Clear filters. The row is always shown, so the table doesn't move when a filter is added.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Lists of values</Heading>
<TextBlock>An enum or bool column filters by a list of checkboxes, one for each value. The user includes a value by checking it and excludes it by clearing it. With every box checked the column isn't filtered; with none checked, no rows match.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filter-lists" />
<TextBlock>A text column can filter by a list of its values too. To fill the list from <Code>Items</Code>, set <Code>FilterList</Code>. To load them yourself, for example from a database, set <Code>FilterValues</Code>; <Code>FilterValuesRequest.ToResultAsync</Code> runs the query for you.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filter-values" />
<TextBlock>Each list shows only the values the other filters leave. A checked value stays in the list, so the user can clear it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Filters in the URL</Heading>
<TextBlock>The table keeps each filter in the URL, so a filtered view can be bookmarked or shared, and filtering works <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor>. A text filter appears as <Code>?f-customer=ada</Code>, and a number range as two keys: <Code>?f-total=100&amp;f-total-to=500</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>When the table has a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code>, the keys start with it. The <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/filtering#ex-table-filters">Column filters</Anchor> example uses the prefix <Code>orders</Code>, so its key is <Code>?orders-f-customer=ada</Code>. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/table#several-tables-on-a-page">Several tables on a page</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For filters that aren't tied to a column, place a <Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor> above the table.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Choosing an operator</Heading>
<TextBlock>An operator sets how a filter compares values, such as contains, equals or between. To let the user choose the operator in a column's filter, or to limit the operators it offers, set <Code>FilterOperators</Code>. DataGrid columns offer the choice by default.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filter-operators" />
<TextBlock>The first operator in the list is the default. A condition with an operator that isn't in the list matches nothing, even when someone types it into the URL. When the user picks an operator other than the default, the URL includes it: <Code>?f-total-op=between&amp;f-total=100&amp;f-total-to=500</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Filters in code</Heading>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, <Code>@@bind-Filter</Code> reads and sets the table's filter from C#. The value is a <Code>Filter</Code> that joins the condition of every filtered column with and.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@BoundFilter</Code>
<TextBlock>A condition that a column's filter can show appears in that filter, as if the user had set it. Any other condition, such as two conditions joined by or, still applies and appears as a tag below the rows. Removing the tag removes the condition.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Anchor Href="docs/common/data#operators">Data and filters</Anchor> lists the operators, and <Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api#filters-as-text">Data from an API</Anchor> the filter syntax. <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data">Loading data</Anchor> shows how a column's condition reaches the database.</TextBlock>

@code {
    private const string BoundFilter = """
        <Table Items="orders" @bind-Filter="filter">...</Table>

        @code {
            // Shown in the Status column's filter: Paid is checked.
            Filter? filter = Filter.In("status", ["Paid"]);

            // No column's filter can show an or, so this one is shown as a tag.
            void PaidOrLarge() => filter = Filter.Any(
                Filter.In("status", ["Paid"]),
                Filter.Where("total", FilterOperator.GreaterThanOrEqual, 1000));
        }
        """;
}
